  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On June 27, US naval and air forces struck five Iranian coastal installations in and around the Strait of Hormuz — including facilities at Sirik and on Qeshm island — citing Iran's non-compliance with Article 5 of the Islamabad MOU's navigation-clearance provisions, according to US official statements carried by PBS News and CBS News; the strike package represented the first direct US action against Iranian territory since the Islamabad MOU was signed June 15. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span></p>
      <p>On June 28, Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps responded with ballistic missiles and drones against US Ali Al Salem Air Base in Kuwait and the US Fifth Fleet facility at Naval Support Activity Bahrain; CNN and Al Jazeera described the exchange as the most direct US-Iran military confrontation since the Hormuz closure began, with multiple detonations confirmed by the US Department of Defense, and the IRGC separately issued a statement characterizing the retaliation as "a proportionate response within the framework of international law" (CNN, Al Jazeera, June 28). → moved <span class="cite">F8</span></p>
      <p>Foreign Minister Araghchi stated on June 28 that "further US strikes will lead to the complete halt of negotiations," while VP Vance separately described the Lucerne technical track as having made "great progress in just the last few hours" in remarks to reporters the same day (PBS News, CBS News, June 28); Pakistan and Qatar foreign ministries issued working-level statements calling for restraint, and the Lucerne follow-on technical session previously scheduled for early July had not been officially cancelled as of June 29. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F10</span></p>
      <p>JMIC widened a navigational corridor near Oman on June 27 and CENTCOM-confirmed mine-clearance operations — which commenced June 16 under the Islamabad MOU demining clause — continued as of June 29; the container vessel MV Ever Lovely was struck by an Iranian drone in the Strait on June 26, and INTERTANKO confirmed as of June 16 that no section of the Traffic Separation Scheme had been declared mine-free; IMF PortWatch recorded approximately 5 commercial transits per day as of June 21, against a pre-war daily average of approximately 93 (Fortune, gCaptain, June 23). → moved <span class="cite">F9</span></p>
      <p>In Israel, the Knesset dissolution bill remained at its first reading (106-0, June 2) as of June 29; the Knesset plenum had scheduled no second or third reading as of that date, with Shas pressing for a September election date and UTJ insisting on October — a gap that blocked any scheduling motion (Haaretz, June 26-29); a Haaretz poll published June 26 projected the governing coalition at approximately 52 seats and the opposition bloc at approximately 58 seats, while the formal Knesset vote count still gave the coalition its majority. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:50%;">Escalation 50%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:35%;">Protracted 35%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:15%;">De-esc 15%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (50%):</strong> The active US-Iran military exchange, Araghchi's explicit halt threshold, and the unresolved Article 5 MOU dispute make further deterioration the modal path if any new US or Iranian strike occurs before the July 2 F8 horizon.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (35%):</strong> Pakistan and Qatar mediators hold the Lucerne track in a reduced form; talks become intermittent rather than formally halted; Hormuz clearing proceeds well below the 50/day threshold; neither side seeks full escalation nor full resolution.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (15%):</strong> Iran does not formally suspend talks (F8 resolves NO), IAEA inspectors arrive by July 10 (F10 resolves YES), no further US strikes occur, and Hormuz transit trends toward recovery — all of which requires the June 27-28 exchange to remain a contained episode rather than a turning point.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media framed the June 27-28 exchange as US military aggression rather than a response to MOU non-compliance: IRNA's headline "American piracy in Persian Gulf waters" ran on June 28, and Tasnim News Agency published a chronology of what it characterized as US violations of the Islamabad MOU's Article 5 navigation provisions; the IRGC Navy communiqué, transmitted via IRNA, described the ballistic-missile and drone strikes on Kuwait and Bahrain as "a proportionate response within the framework of international law." → supports <span class="cite">F8</span></p>
      <p>Araghchi's June 28 IRNA statement used conditional language — "further strikes will lead to" a halt, rather than declaring an immediate suspension — a formulation that maintained a rhetorical threshold without announcing one; Fars News Agency carried the statement prominently, while Kayhan published an editorial arguing the Lucerne roadmap had been rendered void by the June 27 strikes, and Tasnim's editorial desk called for "decisive cessation" of the negotiations. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span></p>
      <p>Iranian state media produced no IRNA, IRIB, or Press TV report confirming or scheduling an IAEA inspector visit as of June 29 — a pattern consistent with past practice of not validating MOU implementation items through public channels prior to completion; a single Tasnim item quoted an unnamed "senior diplomat" stating the IAEA access question remained "under internal review." → supports <span class="cite">F10</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

